{"verify_memory_ids":["memory_86","memory_31"],"intended_action":"simplify_onboarding","scale":"standard_experiment","uses_memory_ids":["memory_86","memory_31","memory_73","memory_44","memory_91"],"downstream_value_risk_flagged":true,"preserves_uncertainty":true,"rationale":"The two declining metrics (onboarding completion -4%, activation -6%) point directly at the funnel stage that memory_86 and memory_31 both say is responsive to intervention, so onboarding simplification is the direction with the best fit between the observed problem and prior evidence. Promotional pricing is the emotionally obvious answer to the competitor's entry pricing, but memory_73 carries an explicit scoped warning: it lifted acquisition and revenue while materially reducing 30-day retention, and explicitly says not to generalize it as a growth strategy. Against a primary objective of sustainable long-term qualified revenue, that is close to a disqualifier, not a tradeoff to rediscover. Referral incentives (memory_57) are sound but address established customers, not the leaking early funnel; enterprise_sales_assist (memory_44) is about a different segment than the one under attack; activation_messaging (memory_91) is the strong runner-up and would be my fallback if verification undermines the onboarding record.

I spend both verification pulls on the memories I am actually about to act on rather than on the pricing warning. memory_86 and memory_31 are adjacent claims that may in fact be one underlying effect recorded twice (day 12 signup-field reduction, day 52 onboarding shortening); if the archive shows they are the same experiment re-consolidated, my evidence base is half as thick as the memory list makes it look. I also want to know whether either lift was measured on retained/qualified accounts or only on first-week activation counts. Verifying memory_73 would be lower value: a warning that was recorded with that much specificity is the kind of thing I should respect without re-litigating, since the cost of being wrong about it is asymmetric.

Downstream value risk: memory_31's mechanism is cutting required signup fields, which is exactly the data that feeds lead qualification and the sales-assisted path memory_44 credits with better pipeline quality. So a real failure mode here is a clean activation lift that quietly degrades qualified revenue downstream — the metric I am optimizing for. I therefore run this as a standard experiment with a holdout and instrument it on downstream qualification and 30-day retention, not just completion and first-week activation, and I hold the field reductions to ones that do not strip enterprise-routing signal. Confidence is moderate rather than high because the inherited records are summaries whose measurement windows I cannot yet see, and because I do not know whether the activation decline is a funnel-mechanics problem or the downstream shadow of the competitor taking the price-sensitive segment that used to populate the top of this funnel — if the latter, an onboarding fix will underperform and the diagnosis, not the lever, was wrong.","confidence":66}
